{"archetypes":[{"name":"fallback-schema-parity","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan proposes self-composed fallback bodies that hard-code a bullet schema claimed to mirror render-run-summary.sh output; repo evidence must confirm the field list and --skill-gating actually match the renderer's current logic before the plan can claim parity.","prompt_body":"Inspect render-run-summary.sh to extract the exact bullet list it emits for --skill implement versus --skill design invocations, then compare against the plan's claimed fallback schema (Mode/Path/Duration/Cost/Issue/PR/Plan review/Code review/OOS filed/Exec issues/Warnings/Run logs plus the larch:run-summary sentinel). Identify any bullets the plan omits, names incorrectly, or claims are --skill-design-only that the renderer actually emits for both skills, or vice-versa. Verify whether test-render-run-summary.sh or any other CI script currently pins this exact schema so that a future renderer-schema change would break the hard-coded fallback silently rather than loudly. Check whether the plan's instruction to omit PR and Code-review bullets for --skill design matches the renderer's actual conditional logic at that flag boundary, or whether the renderer uses a different mechanism (e.g., an explicit --no-pr flag, a skill-name branch, or a missing-arg default) that the self-composed body would need to replicate. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"test-path-injection","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan forces render-run-summary.sh failure via PATH manipulation or stub substitution; repo evidence must confirm the existing harness infrastructure supports that isolation pattern without cross-contaminating other binaries or sourced helpers called in the same test function.","prompt_body":"Examine the existing test-write-final-report.sh and test-render-final-summary.sh harnesses to determine whether they currently employ any PATH isolation, stub-injection, or mock-script pattern, and note whether a shared lib-test.sh or fixture directory already exists that the new test cases should use instead of ad-hoc approaches. Check whether setting PATH='' inside a test function would break other binaries the same function invokes (e.g., mktemp, date, printf, or sourced helpers that shell out), and whether the scripts locate render-run-summary.sh via a PATH lookup, a relative path, or an absolute variable, because the correct injection technique differs for each. Verify whether the plan's per-agent-breakdown happy-path assertion (checking for all of '💰 TOTAL', 'Claude $', 'Codex $', 'Cursor $', 'Tokens: ' on a single bullet line) requires the test harness to produce a valid token-report.json with all three BUCKETS_ blocks populated, and confirm whether the existing test fixtures cover that shape or whether new fixtures must be authored. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
